# Normalise all text files to LF in the repository.
#
# Why: Windows tooling here writes CRLF (ruff format, and Python's default text-mode
# write translation), while other tools write LF. Mixing the two makes git report
# whole-file rewrites for one-line changes, which hides real diffs. The backend also
# deploys to Linux, so LF is the correct storage form.
#
# "text=auto" lets git detect text vs binary; "eol=lf" forces LF in the index AND in
# the working tree on every platform, so a Windows checkout does not reintroduce CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files: never normalise, never attempt a textual diff.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.bmp binary
*.pdf binary

*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary

*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.bz2 binary
*.7z binary

*.pyc binary
*.pdb binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary

# SVG is text (XML) and benefits from real diffs, so it is intentionally left to the
# text=auto rule above rather than marked binary.

# Evaluation fixtures are data: stored byte-for-byte, never line-ending normalised,
# so their case hashes and the diff/snapshot line check are identical on every OS.
eval/cases/** -text
